title('2  First-order effect accuracy')
para('For each type, predict the signed effect as strength times its baseline attenuation derivative. The exact effect is the matched intervention DSI minus baseline. No calibration, clipping, integrated gradients or adaptation under intervention is added. Each row pools at most1,040 type/subtype/speed effects, nested within one model; these are not independent model samples.')
table(['Model','Split','s','MAE','Bias','Rank','Precision','Recall'],[[r['model'],r['split'],str(r['strength']),fmt(r['methods']['first_order']['mae']),fmt(r['methods']['first_order']['signed_bias']),fmt(r['methods']['first_order']['mean_spearman']),fmt(r['methods']['first_order']['precision_at_010']),fmt(r['methods']['first_order']['recall_at_010'])] for r in summary],[40,75,30,61,61,61,82,82])
para('Precision and recall use the fixed0.10 threshold. A good ranking need not imply accurate effect sizes or correct importance labels. Invalid comparisons and undefined denominators are reported, not silently scored as failures.')
table(['Model','s','Exact important','Predicted important','True positive','Valid effects','Near-peak units /16'],[[r['model'],str(r['strength']),r['methods']['first_order']['exact_important'],r['methods']['first_order']['predicted_important'],r['methods']['first_order']['true_positives'],r['methods']['first_order']['n_valid'],r['near_peak_readout_speed_units_conservative']] for r in summary],[44,32,80,96,80,78,82])
para('Near-peak flags conservatively identify any positive temporal peak with a runner-up within1e-6 model-voltage units, across either polarity; some flagged components may be inactive in the selected DSI. Exact temporal ties and denominator near-ties (absolute tolerance1e-7, relative tolerance1e-6) are separately preserved. These estimates are outputs of a specified branch/subgradient algorithm, not certified stable smooth sensitivities.',True)
